1	Wheat Stem Rust	A devastating fungal disease that attacks the stems and leaves of wheat.	\N	Fungal pathogen Puccinia graminis f. sp. tritici.	Apply systemic fungicides (e.g., Tilt, Bayleton).	Use resistant varieties and early planting.	Elongated reddish-brown pustules on stems and leaves.	Wheat, Barley	disease_images/Screenshot_325.png	Wheat Stem Rust	አገዳ ዋግ (የስንዴ ዋግ)	ናይ ስርናይ ዋጊ (ኣገዳ ዋጊ)	Wagii Jirmaa Qamadii	A devastating fungal disease that attacks the stems and leaves of wheat.	የስንዴ አገዳን እና ቅጠልን የሚያጠቃ አደገኛ የፈንገስ በሽታ ነው።	ንኣገዳን ቆጽሊ ስርናይን ዘጥቅዕ ሓደገኛ ናይ ፈንገስ ሕማም እዩ።	Vaayirasii jirmaa fi kalla qamadii irratti miidhaa guddaa geessisuudha.	\N	\N	\N	\N	Fungal pathogen Puccinia graminis f. sp. tritici.	ፑሲኒያ ግራሚኒስ (Puccinia graminis) የተባለ ፈንገስ።	ፑሲኒያ ግራሚኒስ (Puccinia graminis) ዝተባሃለ ፈንገስ።	Fangaasii 'Puccinia graminis' jedhamuun dhufa.	Apply systemic fungicides (e.g., Tilt, Bayleton).	ስልታዊ የፈንገስ ማጥፊያዎችን (ለምሳሌ ቲልት፣ ባይሌተን) መጠቀም።	ስልታዊ ናይ ፈንገስ መጥፍኢታት (ንኣብነት ቲልት፡ ባይሌተን) ተጠቐሙ።	Qoricha farra fangaasii (fkn Tilt, Bayleton) fayyadamuu.	Use resistant varieties and early planting.	ተከላካይ የሆኑ ዝርያዎችን መጠቀምና ቀድሞ መዝራት።	ተጻወርቲ ዓሌታት ምጥቃምን ቀዲምካ ምዝራእን።	Sanyii dandamattoo fayyadamuu fi yeroon facaasuu.	Elongated reddish-brown pustules on stems and leaves.	በአገዳውና በቅጠሉ ላይ ረዘም ያሉ ቀይ-ቡናማ ነጥቦች ወይም ሽፍታዎች ይታያሉ።	ኣብ ኣገዳን ቆጽሊን ዝተረዘሙ ቀይሕ-ቡናማ ነጥብታት ወይ ሽፍታታት ይርኣዩ።	Jirmaa fi kalla irratti mallattoo bifa diimaa-bunaa dheerataa ta'e agarsiisa.
3	Septoria Tritici Blotch	A foliar fungal disease caused by Zymoseptoria tritici that reduces green leaf area and grain filling, common in high-rainfall wheat clusters.	Protect the top two leaves during stem elongation to booting, as these drive most of the yield.	Caused by Zymoseptoria tritici. Favoured by prolonged leaf wetness, frequent rainfall and dense canopies; spread by rain-splashed spores.	Apply protectant and systemic fungicides (triazoles, SDHIs) targeting the flag leaf, following local resistance-management guidance to preserve efficacy.	Use resistant varieties, rotate crops, bury infected residue, avoid excessive seeding rates, and balance nitrogen to reduce canopy density.	Irregular light-brown to greyish lesions containing small black fruiting bodies (pycnidia) visible as tiny dots. Lesions coalesce and kill leaves from the lower to the upper canopy.	Wheat (bread and durum)	disease_images/teddy_afro.jpg	Septoria Tritici Blotch	\N	\N	\N	A foliar fungal disease caused by Zymoseptoria tritici that reduces green leaf area and grain filling, common in high-rainfall wheat clusters.				Protect the top two leaves during stem elongation to booting, as these drive most of the yield.	\N	\N	\N	Caused by Zymoseptoria tritici. Favoured by prolonged leaf wetness, frequent rainfall and dense canopies; spread by rain-splashed spores.	\N	\N	\N	Apply protectant and systemic fungicides (triazoles, SDHIs) targeting the flag leaf, following local resistance-management guidance to preserve efficacy.	\N	\N	\N	Use resistant varieties, rotate crops, bury infected residue, avoid excessive seeding rates, and balance nitrogen to reduce canopy density.	\N	\N	\N	Irregular light-brown to greyish lesions containing small black fruiting bodies (pycnidia) visible as tiny dots. Lesions coalesce and kill leaves from the lower to the upper canopy.			
2	Wheat Stripe Rust (Yellow Rust)	A fungal disease caused by Puccinia striiformis f. sp. tritici, one of the most damaging wheat diseases in the Ethiopian highlands, especially under cool, moist conditions.	Scout fields weekly from tillering to flowering and act quickly when the first stripes appear to protect yield.	Caused by the fungus Puccinia striiformis f. sp. tritici. Favoured by cool temperatures (10-18 C), high humidity and dew, and susceptible varieties. Spores are wind-dispersed over long distances.	Apply registered triazole fungicides (e.g. tebuconazole, propiconazole) at first appearance of pustules and repeat as advised, timing applications to protect the flag leaf.	Grow resistant or tolerant varieties, use certified disease-free seed, avoid late planting, remove volunteer wheat, and monitor fields regularly during the cool season.	Yellow-orange pustules (uredinia) arranged in narrow stripes between the leaf veins, mainly on leaves but also on sheaths, glumes and awns. Severely infected leaves yellow and dry prematurely.	Wheat (bread and durum), barley, triticale	disease_images/IMG_20250506_222205_246.jpg	Wheat Stripe Rust (Yellow Rust)	\N	\N	\N	A fungal disease caused by Puccinia striiformis f. sp. tritici, one of the most damaging wheat diseases in the Ethiopian highlands, especially under cool, moist conditions.				Scout fields weekly from tillering to flowering and act quickly when the first stripes appear to protect yield.	\N	\N	\N	Caused by the fungus Puccinia striiformis f. sp. tritici. Favoured by cool temperatures (10-18 C), high humidity and dew, and susceptible varieties. Spores are wind-dispersed over long distances.	\N	\N	\N	Apply registered triazole fungicides (e.g. tebuconazole, propiconazole) at first appearance of pustules and repeat as advised, timing applications to protect the flag leaf.	\N	\N	\N	Grow resistant or tolerant varieties, use certified disease-free seed, avoid late planting, remove volunteer wheat, and monitor fields regularly during the cool season.	\N	\N	\N	Yellow-orange pustules (uredinia) arranged in narrow stripes between the leaf veins, mainly on leaves but also on sheaths, glumes and awns. Severely infected leaves yellow and dry prematurely.			
\.
